/*
 * Wrangler configuration.
 *
 * The Astro Cloudflare adapter generates the full deploy configuration at build
 * time into `dist/server/wrangler.json` — the server output, not the client one,
 * which this comment named incorrectly until Gate 6 (asserted in
 * tests/unit/deployment-config.test.ts, and corrected in the changelog).
 * Running `wrangler deploy` from
 * the repository root works without any flag: Wrangler detects the generated
 * file, prints "Using redirected Wrangler configuration", and uploads from there
 * via the `.wrangler/deploy/config.json` redirect it writes. Verified with
 * `wrangler deploy --dry-run`, and re-checked in CI on every pull request.
 *
 * `account_id` is deliberately absent (D-023). It is optional for Wrangler, the
 * Cloudflare account does not exist yet, and an invented value would not merely
 * be cosmetic: Cloudflare's own troubleshooting guidance for "Incorrect
 * account_id" is to remove it, and a wrong integer deploys against whatever
 * account that identifier belongs to. Workers Builds supplies the account
 * context to its builds, so nothing needs to be committed for the founder to
 * deploy, and a local or CI deploy passes it explicitly instead
 * (see docs/TRL_DEPLOYMENT.md).
 *
 * Two bindings are provisioned automatically by the adapter on first deploy and
 * will appear in that deploy's output: a KV namespace for `SESSION` and an
 * `IMAGES` binding. Neither is used by the current application, which has no
 * sessions and no runtime image processing; they are the adapter's defaults and
 * require no account-scoped identifier in this file.
 *
 * No production values live here: runtime variables are set in the hosting
 * platform, never committed (docs/TRL_SECURITY.md).
 */
{
  "name": "trl-service",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-09-16",
  "observability": {
    "enabled": true
  }

  /*
   * Rate limiting for POST /contact/ (docs/TRL_RATE_LIMITING.md).
   *
   * The enforcing code is committed and tested: the endpoint reads
   * `env.CONTACT_RATE_LIMITER` and fails open when it is absent, which is why
   * local preview and CI work unchanged with this block commented out.
   *
   * It stays commented because `namespace_id` is account-scoped — a positive
   * integer unique to the founder's Cloudflare account, which does not yet
   * exist (D-004/D-006). Committing an invented value would fabricate
   * infrastructure and, worse, would deploy against whatever namespace that
   * integer happened to collide with. Uncomment and set the integer at the
   * deployment gate; nothing else needs to change.
   *
   * "ratelimits": [
   *   {
   *     "name": "CONTACT_RATE_LIMITER",
   *     "namespace_id": "<founder-supplied positive integer, as a string>",
   *     "simple": { "limit": 10, "period": 10 }
   *   }
   * ]
   */
}
